## 4. What "prove the test catches a real defect" taught this session about axe-core's own limits

25 vitest tests for this slice, including one deliberately-broken-then-
restored proof on the highest-stakes logic: the "selected but filtered
out" fallback was changed to search the unfiltered `incidents` array
instead of `filtered`, confirmed to fail (the panel then showed a stale
incident that should have been hidden), then reverted.

Separately, this slice's master/detail state had never been reached by
any existing Playwright test (every prior axe/scanner check only visits
each page in its default, nothing-selected state) - a click-triggered
UI state is exactly the kind of new attack surface a once-per-page scan
misses. Added `Incidents master/detail has no serious/critical axe
violations` (both languages) to `accessibility.spec.ts`, which actually
clicks a row's focus link before scanning. Both pass.

Then, following this session's own established discipline, tried to
prove *that* test catches a real defect too: removed `InspectorPanel`'s
`aria-label` (leaving a bare `role="region"` with no accessible name) and
reran - it still passed, with zero violations reported even without any
severity filter at all. This is not a flaw in the test: axe-core's
`wcag2a`/`wcag2aa` ruleset genuinely does not flag an unnamed generic
`role="region"` as any kind of violation (a well-documented limit of
automated accessibility tooling, distinct from a real screen-reader
pass - the same category as the "NVDA/Orca passes" this project already
lists as an external blocker it cannot fake around). The `aria-label`
was restored anyway: it is still correct practice and worth keeping,
just not something this specific automated check can be credited with
enforcing. Recorded here rather than silently kept as an unexamined
"prove it fails" step that happened not to fail, which would have
overstated what was actually verified.

## 5. Actually looked at it

Fresh browser tab, demo recording, Arabic. Clicked "عرض هذه الحادثة في
لوحة جانبية مع بقية القائمة" (show this incident in a side panel) on the
first incident: the compact list (all 16 incidents, one line each -
time, severity, title, confidence) and the `InspectorPanel` (closing ✕,
the incident's translated title as the panel heading, then its full
`IncidentCard` - chain, "الدليل الخام" evidence disclosures, root cause,
advice, and the "تصدير أدلة هذه الحادثة…" export button, all still
functioning) both rendered, confirmed via `get_page_text`. A screenshot
attempt was inconclusive (an apparent single-column render that did not
match the DOM), so verified the actual layout geometry directly instead
of trusting the image: `getComputedStyle(.incidents-layout).flexDirection`
is `"row"` at this viewport (1280px, above the 899px breakpoint), and
the list/panel `getBoundingClientRect()`s share the same `top` with the
list positioned at the RTL start (`left: 708`) and the panel filling the
rest (`left: 127`, wider) - genuinely side by side, correctly mirrored
for RTL by plain logical flexbox `row` direction with no extra RTL
handling needed. Console clean throughout.

## 6. Phase 5 status

Both halves of "Rebuild Incidents (master/detail, search/sort/filter)"
are now done. Remaining: Rules "match-count in range" and Settings
regrouping - both revisited and found to carry more hidden scope than
their one-line descriptions suggested; written up honestly in the
progress log rather than guessed at carelessly, the same way this log's
§1 and §4 write up InspectorPanel's and axe's own real limits instead of
glossing over them.
